From: Christopher Stenger
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: h713: identity-map the MIPS frame-buffer pool for the display

When the MIPS firmware presents an HDMI source itself, AFBD source 0 fetches
the capture ring inside the firmware's frame-buffer pool (0x4bf41000, 26 MiB,
display_cfg.xml <frame_buffer>) at physical addresses the capture hardware
latches into AFBD +0x320/+0x324. The display's IOMMU master translates from
probe, so without an identity map those fetches fault, and one fault wedges
the AFBD fetch engine for the boot.

Give the pool node iommu-addresses for the display and list it in the
display's memory-region (by name "mips-framebuf"; the driver still allocates
from the system CMA pool, looked up by name "framebuffer"). Add a reg-less
reservation of the same range for the VE, which shares the IOMMU group and
initialises the IOVA allocator first, as iova-reserve-scanout does for the
U-Boot scanout.

Measured on board B (docs/hdmi-panel-source-design-2026-10-23.md 10.11-10.12):
the firmware programs source 0 for capture with no address slots, taking its
addresses from the capture pair inside this pool.

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
@@ -91,10 +91,20 @@
 			no-map;
 		};
 
-		/* The firmware's own framebuffer, per display_cfg.xml frame_buffer */
+		/*
+		 * The firmware's own framebuffer, per display_cfg.xml frame_buffer.
+		 * Every MIPS frame buffer comes from this pool, including the HDMI
+		 * capture ring. When the firmware presents HDMI itself, AFBD source
+		 * 0 fetches that ring at the physical addresses the capture side
+		 * latches into AFBD +0x320/+0x324 -- through the display's IOMMU
+		 * master, which translates from probe. Identity-map the pool for
+		 * the display so those fetches resolve; iova-reserve-mips-framebuf
+		 * below keeps the shared IOVA allocator out of it.
+		 */
 		mips_framebuf: framebuf@4bf41000 {
 			reg = <0x4bf41000 0x1a00000>;
 			no-map;
+			iommu-addresses = <&display 0x4bf41000 0x1a00000>;
 		};
 
 		/*
@@ -148,6 +158,15 @@
 		ve_scanout_iova: iova-reserve-scanout {
 			iommu-addresses = <&ve 0x6c100000 0x800000>;
 		};
+
+		/*
+		 * The firmware pool again, as an IOVA reservation for the VE, for
+		 * the reason iova-reserve-scanout above gives: the shared domain's
+		 * allocator is populated by whichever device initialises it first.
+		 */
+		ve_mips_framebuf_iova: iova-reserve-mips-framebuf {
+			iommu-addresses = <&ve 0x4bf41000 0x1a00000>;
+		};
 	};
 
 	psci {
@@ -1406,7 +1425,7 @@
 			 * of_dma_set_restricted_buffer() only fires for a
 			 * "restricted-dma-pool" compatible, which this is not.
 			 */
-			memory-region = <&ve_scanout_iova>;
+			memory-region = <&ve_scanout_iova>, <&ve_mips_framebuf_iova>;
 			status = "okay";
 		};
 
@@ -1899,8 +1918,8 @@
 			 * the identity map below.
 			 */
 			iommus = <&iommu 2 1>;
-			memory-region = <&framebuf_reserved>;
-			memory-region-names = "adopted-scanout";
+			memory-region = <&framebuf_reserved>, <&mips_framebuf>;
+			memory-region-names = "adopted-scanout", "mips-framebuf";
 			status = "okay";
 		};
 
